# Tracing `new Chalk({level: 3}).red("foo")` vs `new Chalk({level: 0}).red("foo")`

## 0. Setup: how `.red` becomes a callable builder

In `source/index.js`, at construction time `Chalk`'s constructor just delegates to `chalkFactory`, which creates the `chalk` function and calls `applyOptions` to set `chalk.level` (index.js:34-48, see §1 below for `applyOptions`).

Every named color (`red`, `green`, `blue`, …) is defined as a lazy getter on `styles`, built from `ansiStyles`'s entries:

```js
// source/index.js:56-64
for (const [styleName, style] of Object.entries(ansiStyles)) {
	styles[styleName] = {
		get() {
			const builder = createBuilder(this, createStyler(style.open, style.close, this[STYLER]), this[IS_EMPTY]);
			Object.defineProperty(this, styleName, {value: builder});
			return builder;
		},
	};
}
```

`ansiStyles.red` is `{open: '[31m', close: '[39m'}` (see §3). So accessing `.red` builds a *styler* object holding the literal open/close ANSI codes for red, then wraps it in a callable `builder` via `createBuilder` (index.js:152-166). Calling `builder("foo")` invokes `applyStyle(builder, "foo")` (index.js:155, 168).

Note: for named colors like `red`, the open/close codes are fixed ANSI-16 sequences baked in at getter-definition time — the `levelMapping` array (§2) is *not* consulted for these, only for the dynamic `rgb`/`hex`/`ansi256`/`bgRgb`/`bgHex`/`bgAnsi256` methods. What *is* level-gated for every style, including `red`, is whether `applyStyle` emits the codes at all — that's §1.

## 1. Where `level` gates whether escape codes are emitted at all

`applyStyle` is the function every builder calls when invoked with a string, and it starts with a hard gate on `self.level`:

```js
// source/index.js:168-171
const applyStyle = (self, string) => {
	if (self.level <= 0 || !string) {
		return self[IS_EMPTY] ? '' : string;
	}
	...
```

`self.level` resolves through the `level` accessor defined on `proto`:

```js
// source/index.js:121-129
level: {
	enumerable: true,
	get() {
		return this[GENERATOR].level;
	},
	set(level) {
		this[GENERATOR].level = level;
	},
},
```

`this[GENERATOR]` is the original chalk instance (`self` in `createBuilder`, index.js:161: `builder[GENERATOR] = self;`), whose `.level` was set by `applyOptions`:

```js
// source/index.js:24-32
const applyOptions = (object, options = {}) => {
	if (options.level && !(Number.isInteger(options.level) && options.level >= 0 && options.level <= 3)) {
		throw new Error('The `level` option should be an integer from 0 to 3');
	}

	// Detect level if not set manually
	const colorLevel = stdoutColor ? stdoutColor.level : 0;
	object.level = options.level === undefined ? colorLevel : options.level;
};
```

For `new Chalk({level: 3})`, `object.level` is set to `3`. For `new Chalk({level: 0})`, it's set to `0`.

- With `level = 3`: `self.level <= 0` is `false`, so `applyStyle` falls through, reads `self[STYLER]` (the `{open: '[31m', close: '[39m', openAll, closeAll}` styler built in §0), and at the end returns `openAll + string + closeAll` (index.js:199) — i.e. `'[31m' + 'foo' + '[39m'`.
- With `level = 0`: `self.level <= 0` is `true`, so `applyStyle` immediately returns `self[IS_EMPTY] ? '' : string` — since `.red` is a color style (not the `visible` no-op style), `IS_EMPTY` is `false`/falsy for it, so it returns `string` unchanged, i.e. the plain `"foo"`. **No styler is even consulted, no ANSI codes are built.**

This single `if (self.level <= 0 ...)` check at index.js:169 is the gate for *all* styles, not just RGB/hex ones — it's what makes `level: 0` behave as a global "no color" switch.

## 2. How `levelMapping` picks the color model (ansi / ansi256 / ansi16m)

```js
// source/index.js:14-20
// `supportsColor.level` → `ansiStyles.color[name]` mapping
const levelMapping = [
	'ansi',
	'ansi',
	'ansi256',
	'ansi16m',
];
```

This array is indexed directly by the numeric `level` (0-3) and is used only by the dynamic color-model getters (`rgb`, `hex`, `ansi256`, and their `bg*` counterparts), not by the static named colors like `red`:

```js
// source/index.js:96-117
for (const model of usedModels) {          // ['rgb', 'hex', 'ansi256']
	styles[model] = {
		get() {
			const {level} = this;
			return function (...arguments_) {
				const styler = createStyler(getModelAnsi(model, levelMapping[level], 'color', ...arguments_), ansiStyles.color.close, this[STYLER]);
				return createBuilder(this, styler, this[IS_EMPTY]);
			};
		},
	};

	const bgModel = 'bg' + model[0].toUpperCase() + model.slice(1);
	styles[bgModel] = {
		get() {
			const {level} = this;
			return function (...arguments_) {
				const styler = createStyler(getModelAnsi(model, levelMapping[level], 'bgColor', ...arguments_), ansiStyles.bgColor.close, this[STYLER]);
				return createBuilder(this, styler, this[IS_EMPTY]);
			};
		},
	};
}
```

`levelMapping[level]` turns the numeric level into one of the strings `'ansi'`, `'ansi256'`, or `'ansi16m'`, which is passed as the `level` parameter into `getModelAnsi`:

```js
// source/index.js:74-92
const getModelAnsi = (model, level, type, ...arguments_) => {
	if (model === 'rgb') {
		if (level === 'ansi16m') {
			return ansiStyles[type].ansi16m(...arguments_);
		}

		if (level === 'ansi256') {
			return ansiStyles[type].ansi256(ansiStyles.rgbToAnsi256(...arguments_));
		}

		return ansiStyles[type].ansi(ansiStyles.rgbToAnsi(...arguments_));
	}

	if (model === 'hex') {
		return getModelAnsi('rgb', level, type, ...ansiStyles.hexToRgb(...arguments_));
	}

	return ansiStyles[type][model](...arguments_);
};
```

So e.g. `.rgb(255,0,0)` at level 3 → `levelMapping[3] === 'ansi16m'` → calls `ansiStyles.color.ansi16m(255,0,0)` directly; at level 1 → `levelMapping[1] === 'ansi'` → downsamples via `rgbToAnsi` first. Levels 0 and 1 both map to `'ansi'` (levelMapping[0] === levelMapping[1] === 'ansi'), which is harmless because at level 0 `applyStyle`'s gate (§1) already returns the plain string before any styler/codes are used — the model lookup happens but its result is simply discarded.

## 3. Where the per-level ANSI generators live in `source/vendor/ansi-styles/index.js`

The three wrapper-generator factories, parameterized by a foreground/background `offset`, are:

```js
// source/vendor/ansi-styles/index.js:1-7
const ANSI_BACKGROUND_OFFSET = 10;

const wrapAnsi16 = (offset = 0) => code => `[${code + offset}m`;

const wrapAnsi256 = (offset = 0) => code => `[${38 + offset};5;${code}m`;

const wrapAnsi16m = (offset = 0) => (red, green, blue) => `[${38 + offset};2;${red};${green};${blue}m`;
```

They're installed onto `styles.color` / `styles.bgColor` inside `assembleStyles()`:

```js
// source/vendor/ansi-styles/index.js:102-107
styles.color.ansi = wrapAnsi16();
styles.color.ansi256 = wrapAnsi256();
styles.color.ansi16m = wrapAnsi16m();
styles.bgColor.ansi = wrapAnsi16(ANSI_BACKGROUND_OFFSET);
styles.bgColor.ansi256 = wrapAnsi256(ANSI_BACKGROUND_OFFSET);
styles.bgColor.ansi16m = wrapAnsi16m(ANSI_BACKGROUND_OFFSET);
```

These are exactly the functions `getModelAnsi` in index.js calls as `ansiStyles[type].ansi16m(...)`, `ansiStyles[type].ansi256(...)`, `ansiStyles[type].ansi(...)` (`type` being `'color'` or `'bgColor'`). Separately, the fixed per-name codes for the *named* colors (`red` = `[31, 39]`, etc., used directly by `.red`) live in the `styles.color` / `styles.bgColor` / `styles.modifier` tables at ansi-styles/index.js:9-65, and get turned into `{open, close}` escape-code pairs during the same `assembleStyles()` pass at lines 78-81 (`open: '[${style[0]}m'`, `close: '[${style[1]}m'`).

## 4. How `source/vendor/supports-color/index.js` computes the default level with no options

`applyOptions` (index.js:30) reads `stdoutColor ? stdoutColor.level : 0`, where `stdoutColor` is `supportsColor.stdout` imported from `#supports-color` (index.js:1, 8). That object is built at module load with no options at all:

```js
// source/vendor/supports-color/index.js:185-188
const supportsColor = {
	stdout: createSupportsColor({isTTY: tty.isatty(1)}),
	stderr: createSupportsColor({isTTY: tty.isatty(2)}),
};
