```

`createSupportsColor` calls the internal `_supportsColor(stream, options)` and wraps the numeric result with `translateLevel`:

```js
// source/vendor/supports-color/index.js:176-183
export function createSupportsColor(stream, options = {}) {
	const level = _supportsColor(stream, {
		streamIsTTY: stream && stream.isTTY,
		...options,
	});

	return translateLevel(level);
}
```

`_supportsColor` (lines 60-174) is a cascade of environment/flag checks run in order — CLI flags (`--color`/`--no-color`) → `FORCE_COLOR` env var → CI-specific vars (Azure DevOps, `CI`, `TEAMCITY_VERSION`) → non-TTY short-circuit to `0` → `TERM=dumb` → Windows build-number check → `COLORTERM`/`TERM_PROGRAM`/`TERM` pattern matching — finally falling back to `min` (`forceColor || 0`) at line 173 if nothing matched. Whatever integer (0-3) falls out of that cascade is passed to:

```js
// source/vendor/supports-color/index.js:47-58
function translateLevel(level) {
	if (level === 0) {
		return false;
	}

	return {
		level,
		hasBasic: true,
		has256: level >= 2,
		has16m: level >= 3,
	};
}
```

So `supportsColor.stdout` is either `false` (level 0, e.g. non-TTY stdout with no force flags) or an object with a numeric `.level`. Back in `applyOptions`, `stdoutColor ? stdoutColor.level : 0` handles both cases: if `false`, the ternary falls to plain `0`; otherwise it reads `.level` off that object. This is exactly what `new Chalk()` (no options passed) would default to — `new Chalk({level: 3})` and `new Chalk({level: 0})` instead override this entirely since `options.level !== undefined` in both cases (index.js:31).

## 5. How `source/utilities.js` stops ANSI codes leaking across an embedded newline

After computing `openAll`/`closeAll` for the full nested styler chain, `applyStyle` checks for a newline and, if found, hands off to `stringEncaseCRLFWithFirstIndex`:

```js
// source/index.js:191-199
