		const gotCR = string[index - 1] === '\r';
		returnValue += string.slice(endIndex, (gotCR ? index - 1 : index)) + prefix + (gotCR ? '\r\n' : '\n') + postfix;
		endIndex = index + 1;
		index = string.indexOf('\n', endIndex);
	} while (index !== -1);

	returnValue += string.slice(endIndex);
	return returnValue;
}
```

Here `prefix` is `closeAll` and `postfix` is `openAll` (per the call site above). So for `chalk.red("hello\nworld")`, the string becomes `hello` + `closeAll` + `\n` + `openAll` + `world`, and the outer `applyStyle` wraps the whole thing once more in `openAll + ... + closeAll`. The net effect: the color is explicitly closed right before every line break and reopened right after, so a naive terminal/pager that resets styling at each line boundary (the macOS bleed bug referenced in the comment, chalk/chalk#92) can't let the color escape past the line it was applied to — every line carries its own matching open/close pair instead of relying on one open code at the very start bleeding through multiple lines.

## 6. Existing test pinning level-0 plain-string behavior

`test/chalk.js` (lines 108-111):

```js
test('don\'t emit RGB codes if level is 0', t => {
	t.is(new Chalk({level: 0}).hex('#FF0000')('hello'), 'hello');
	t.is(new Chalk({level: 0}).bgHex('#FF0000')('hello'), 'hello');
});
```

This asserts that with `level: 0`, both `.hex(...)` and `.bgHex(...)` styling collapse to the untouched input string `'hello'` — the same `self.level <= 0` short-circuit in `applyStyle` (index.js:169-171, §1) that would make `new Chalk({level: 0}).red("foo")` return the plain `"foo"`.
